    "evidence_text": "We have also\n\nRelated Work\tBahdanau et al.  introduced attention first in neural machine translation.\n\nthis except we use element wise difference instead of addition to build our conflict function.\n\nTheir experiments have revolved around three attention functions - dot, concat and general.\n\nTheir findings include that dot product works best for global attention.\n\nAttention has been widely used in pair-classification problems like natural language inference.\n\nThis works very similar to how attention works but inversely.\n\nThe remaining of the process acts similar to how attention works.\n\nThe parameters of each attention and projections are different in each head.\n\nFinally, they concatenate all the attentions which is similar to how we concatenate conflict and attention.\n\nHowever, they use dot-product to compute each of the attention.\n\nOur conflict head explicitly captures difference between the inputs.\n\nVisualizing attention and conflict\tWe observe how our conflict model learns\n\nthe dissimilarities between word representations.\n\n(3) and (8).\n\nThe encoder is shared among the sequences simply uses two stacked GRU layers.\n\nThe classifier part is simply stacked fully-connected layers.\n\nThis is a popular pair-level classification task on which extensive\n\nclick on a question to view the answer.\n\nWe observe that model with both attention and conflict combined gives the best results.\n\nTask 2 respectively.",
